index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..b38caf7a1522255b31c7e340deadb8fc10c00cd7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-17-add-json-merge-patch/design.md
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+## Context
+
+The library already provides JSON Pointer and JSON Patch in `src/index.ts`, with internal helpers `isObject`, `setMember` (defines own data members, so `__proto__` is safe) and `clone` (deep copy that keeps `__proto__` as data). Inputs are untrusted parsed JSON. Governing standard: RFC 7396 §2 (RFC 7386, named in the request, is obsoleted by it with the same algorithm).
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:** a faithful, pure, prototype-safe implementation of the RFC 7396 MergePatch algorithm with the signature `applyMergePatch(target, patch)`.
+**Non-Goals:** diffing, HTTP/media-type handling, parsing text, configurable array merging.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Follow the RFC pseudocode literally.** If `patch` is not an object, return `clone(patch)`. Otherwise start from a clone of `target` if it is an object, else `{}`; for each own key of `patch`: `null` deletes the key, anything else sets `merge(result[key] if own, value)`. Recursing with the existing value (or `undefined` treated as non-object) gives the RFC behaviour where nulls inside newly added objects are dropped.
+- **Top-level `null` patch returns `null`.** This is what the algorithm says (a non-object patch replaces the target). It is a known sharp edge for callers, noted in the spec.
+- **Arrays are opaque values**, replaced wholesale and cloned with their nulls intact (RFC 7396 §2).
+- **Reuse `clone`, `isObject`, `setMember`; use `Object.hasOwn` and `Object.keys`.** This avoids prototype pollution and inherited-key confusion. Deleting uses `delete` on an own key of our own fresh copy.
+- **Validate JSON-ness up front** with a recursive check, throwing a new `JsonMergePatchError` (same pattern as the existing error classes). The RFC does not address invalid input; this is an addition so `undefined` (which JSON cannot represent and which would otherwise silently act like "no value") fails loudly. Validation happens before any work, so no partial result exists.
+- **No dependencies** and no `package.json` change.
+- Recursion depth is bounded by input depth, as for `clone`; parsed JSON from `JSON.parse` is already depth-limited by the engine's own stack.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- A `null` patch value cannot set a member to `null` (inherent to RFC 7396). Documented, not worked around.
+- Rejecting non-JSON values is stricter than the RFC; acceptable for a contract-oriented library.
